.TH PLANKTON 1 "2026-07-07" "plankton v0" "plankton manual"
.SH NAME
plankton \- content-addressed substrate for reproducible results (fotons)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plankton
.I command
[\fIarguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plankton
records \fIreproducible results\fR: it content-addresses files, records how they
were produced as a signed \fBfoton\fR, and answers lineage/reproduction questions by
hash. It is a metadata plane - it stores no file bytes.
.PP
Two invariants govern everything:
.TP
.B plankton documents, it never executes.
It hashes, verifies, compares, and indexes. It never runs your commands, tools, or
normalisers - those are done by \fIexecutors\fR (separate programs) and plankton merely
records and compares the results by hash.
.TP
.B the kernel is network-free.
Serving a port, mirroring a URL peer, transparency-log anchoring, and byte pinning are
\fInot\fR plankton - they live in the cockpit,
.BR kton (1).
plankton keeps only \fIlocal\fR overlay-by-hash federation.
.SH CONCEPTS
.TP
.B foton
A signed record that a set of input files, under a protocol (a command descriptor), produced
a set of output files - all by content hash. Authored with
.B plankton author
and ingested with
.BR "plankton add" .
.TP
.B ray
The chain of fotons an output passes through, e.g. a raw output followed by a normalisation
foton. Two results are equivalent when their rays \fIconverge\fR (terminal hashes match).
.TP
.B "reproduction identity (L0/L1)"
.B L0
= byte-identical (raw hashes equal).
.B L1
= equal after the same normaliser is applied in the ray. L0 is strictest and implies L1.
The L0/L1 \fInaming\fR is a label applied on top; plankton just reports whether rays converge.
.TP
.B potential
A pure recipe (e.g. "strip license/date lines") - a normaliser \fIdefinition\fR. Applying
it needs an \fIexecutor\fR (never plankton); plankton only compares the resulting fotons.
.TP
.B spectrum
The object that \fIdefines a tool\fR: a set of reference fotons (gold model+data \(-> output)
plus a normaliser potential. A candidate tool \fIfulfils\fR the spectrum when it reproduces
every reference output. Whether that fulfilment is \fIaccepted\fR as a validation, and called
"L1", is a signed
.BR nekton (1)
attestation - not plankton's verdict.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B plankton keygen \fIname\fR [--seed \fI64-hex\fR]
Generate an Ed25519 signing identity: writes \fIname\fR.key (secret) and \fIname\fR.pub.
\fB--seed\fR derives it from a seed rather than the entropy pool, so a corpus or a snapshot rebuilds
to the same record ids - the public key is inside every signed payload, so a random key per run moves
every id. Fixtures only: such a key is exactly as strong as the seed behind it.
.TP
.B plankton pubkey \fIkey.key\fR|\fIhex\fR
Print the public key hex. Needed because an identity can be written by hand (a bare 32-byte seed is a
valid .key), and \fBverify\fR, \fB--trust-keys\fR and viewer key directories all read the public half.
.TP
.B plankton keyid \fIkey.pub\fR|\fIkey.key\fR|\fIhex\fR
Print the keyid shown as \fBby=key:\fR\fI<id>\fR - maps a key back to the signer it identifies.
.TP
.B plankton records [--json] [--since \fIN\fR]
Every record together with its signed envelope: the SPEC Clause 12 \fBsync(since)\fR answer, emitted
on stdout rather than over a transport. \fB--since\fR takes the cursor from the previous answer's
\fBmax\fR. This is the surface a consumer verifies against - a projection like \fBshow\fR does not
carry the bytes a signature stands over.
.TP
.B plankton reproductions [--trust-keys \fIdir\fR] [--json] \fIsha256:output-hash\fR
How many INDEPENDENT signers produced exactly these bytes. With \fB--trust-keys\fR only signers whose
signature actually verifies are counted; without it the count is self-declared and forgeable, and is
flagged as such. The JSON form marks \fBverified\fR per record, not once for the answer.
.TP
.B plankton attach \fIsha256:id\fR --scheme \fIS\fR --file \fIF\fR [--media \fIM\fR]
Bind external verification material to a foton by its content address (SPEC 8.1): a Sigstore bundle,
a Rekor entry, an RFC 3161 token, an X.509 or eIDAS signature. Stored, NEVER evaluated - deciding
which issuers count is a consumer's trust policy. An unknown scheme is carried, not rejected; it only
needs \fB--media\fR so a reader knows how to read the bytes.
.TP
.B plankton material \fIsha256:id\fR [--json]
What verification material is attached to a record. Listed, not verified.
.TP
.B plankton author --in \fIF\fR --out \fIF\fR --cmd \fB"..."\fR [--kind \fIK\fR] [--environment \fIsha256:…\fR] [--sign \fIkey\fR] [-o \fIout\fR] [--add] [--registry \fID\fR]
Author + sign a foton from files that ALREADY EXIST: it hashes each \fB--in\fR/\fB--out\fR file
and RECORDS the \fB--cmd\fR descriptor. It does not run \fB--cmd\fR. Repeat \fB--in\fR/\fB--out\fR
for multiple files. Default output: foton.dsse.json.
.br
\fB--add\fR ingests the foton into the registry in the same step (the common "record it now" case);
with \fB--add\fR and no \fB-o\fR, no intermediate file is written. \fB--registry \fID\fR picks the
target registry (else \fBPLANKTON_DIR\fR, else ./plankton-data).
.br
\fB--environment\fR \fIsha256:…\fR (optional) records an ENV-SPECTRUM id (see \fBplankton spectrum\fR,
\fBenv\fR spectra) the producing environment claims to fulfil. It rides inside the protocol descriptor,
so it is COVERED - part of \fBprotocol.ref\fR and the action key: a foton produced "under a
qualified-X environment" is a distinct computation. It is executor-agnostic (names a qualification, not
a docker image). Concrete environment data (an OCI digest, a lockfile hash) is instead CARRIED, opaque
to the kernel, and never changes the foton id.
.RS
.nf

  plankton author --in data.csv --out fit.txt --cmd "python fit.py data.csv" \\
      --sign me.key -o fit.foton.json
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B plankton author \fIspec.json key.hex out.dsse\fR
The explicit form: author from a JSON spec (inputs/outputs/protocol).
.TP
.B plankton add \fIenvelope.dsse.json\fR [--registry \fID\fR]
Ingest a signed foton into the local registry (default ./plankton-data).
\fBRecords without verifying\fR - a foton with a present-but-unchecked (even tampered) signature
ingests here without complaint; run \fBplankton verify\fR to check authenticity. An \fBunsigned\fR
foton (no signature at all) is rejected, and a foton whose \fBprotocol.ref\fR does not match its
descriptor (SPEC 6.2) is rejected.
.TP
.B plankton verify \fIenvelope.dsse.json\fR \fIpubkey.pub\fR|\fIhex\fR
Verify a DSSE signature against a public key (a \fB.pub file path\fR OR the hex). Always prints the
\fBsigner's keyid\fR (embedded in the record) and \fByour key's keyid\fR, then one of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBVALID\fR (exit 0) - the signature checks out.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWRONG KEY\fR (exit 2) - your keyid differs from the signer's: you verified against the wrong
identity, \fInot\fR a tamper. Supply the signer's actual public key.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTAMPERED\fR (exit 1) - the keyids match but the signature does not: the bytes were altered
after signing.
.RE
\fBverify is the only command that checks signatures.\fR \fBadd\fR, \fBreproduces\fR and
\fBspectrum\fR never do - they trust the hash, not the signer.
.TP
.B plankton show \fIfoton.dsse.json\fR|\fIsha256:id\fR|\fIsha256:outputHash\fR
Print one foton: its command, inputs, and outputs. Accepts a foton envelope file, a foton id, OR an
\fBoutput hash\fR (resolved to the foton that produced it). The command is what was RECORDED;
plankton never ran it.
.TP
.B plankton hash \fIfile\fR
Print a file's content address (sha256:...).
.TP
.B plankton producer [--source \fID\fR ...] \fIsha256:...\fR
Which foton produced this file (lineage join).
.TP
.B plankton uses [--source \fID\fR ...] \fIsha256:...\fR
Which fotons consumed this file (discovery).
.TP
.B plankton lineage [--source \fID\fR ...] \fIsha256:...\fR
Walk producers backwards. Repeat \fB--source\fR to resolve over the \fBunion\fR of several registries
at query time, deduped by content hash, with no copy (SPEC Clause 11) - so two independently-held
stores join at a shared hash without mirroring. Without \fB--source\fR, the single \fBPLANKTON_DIR\fR
is used.
.TP
.B plankton reproduces \fIref cand\fR [--via \fIpotential\fR]
Do two outputs reproduce? \fBL0\fR if raw-equal, \fBL1\fR if equal after the SAME normaliser
\fIpotential\fR (its protocol ref, or a normaliser foton id) has normalised BOTH in the ray.
\fB--via names a potential, not a kind\fR: two different normalisers of the same kind are different
comparisons and must not both satisfy one L1 assertion (SPEC 9). Only L0/L1 are kernel checks;
\fBL2\fR (equal within tolerance) is \fInot\fR computed here - it is a comparator's signed verdict (a
nekton attestation). So \(dqno L0/L1 match - an L2 comparator verdict is required\(dq is a pointer to
that next step, not an error in plankton.
.TP
.B plankton reuse \fIfoton.statement.json\fR
Action key + cache-hit check: has this exact computation (inputs + protocol) been recorded before?
A HIT is a cache-key match, \fBnot a trust decision\fR: the matched foton's recorded output is
untrusted until you \fBplankton verify\fR it against a key you trust. The action key binds to the
protocol descriptor when one is carried; a bare (descriptor-less) ref is namespaced separately so it
cannot collide with a verifiable descriptor.
.TP
.B plankton spectrum define --id \fIid\fR [--of \fB"..."\fR] [--normaliser \fIP\fR] [--member \fIname\fR=sha256:...] [-o \fIfile\fR]
Create or extend a spectrum (a tool definition). Starts as a bare id and grows members. Default
file: \fIid\fR.spectrum.json. Each \fB--member\fR (and \fB--candidate\fR, below) hash is an
\fBoutput hash\fR (the gold/candidate result), not a foton id.
.TP
.B plankton spectrum show \fIspectrum.json\fR
Show a spectrum's normaliser and reference members.
.TP
.B plankton spectrum check \fIspectrum.json\fR --candidate \fIname\fR=sha256:...
Report whether the candidate FULFILS the spectrum (per member: identical, via the potential, or
not). Compares fotons that already exist; runs nothing. Record the "validated at L0/L1" judgment
as a
.BR nekton (1)
attestation.
.TP
.B plankton mirror \fIlocal-registry-dir\fR
Overlay a peer registry (a local directory) into yours by hash - pure, no network. For a URL
peer use
.BR "kton mirror" .
.TP
.B plankton export [--title \fIT\fR] [\fIout\fR]
Serialize the horizon graph as JSON (headless query projection).
.TP
.B plankton export --rdf [--lineage \fIfotonId\fR|\fIoutputHash\fR] [-o \fIout\fR]
Project the foton lineage as RDF (Turtle, PROV-O): each foton a \fBprov:Activity\fR, inputs
\fBprov:used\fR, outputs \fBprov:wasGeneratedBy\fR. Content nodes use the same \fBpk:\fR IRIs as the
nekton nanopublication export, so the two graphs MERGE - a nekton claim about a foton and that
foton's lineage meet at one node, and a consumer can reason across the whole kton (e.g. is there a
reviewed step in a result's lineage?). \fB--lineage\fR restricts output to one record's ancestry (a
subset). A pure offline serialization; plankton documents, it does not reason.
.TP
.B plankton man
Print this manual page (roff source; pipe to \fBman -l -\fR to render). The page is embedded in the
binary - the binary is self-documenting offline.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PLANKTON_DIR
Registry directory (default ./plankton-data).
.SH EXAMPLES
Record a result, then verify a re-run reproduces it after normalisation:
.RS
.nf

  plankton keygen me
  plankton author --in in.csv --out out.lst --cmd "run" --sign me.key -o a.foton.json
  plankton add a.foton.json
  plankton producer $(plankton hash out.lst)      # who made out.lst
  plankton reproduces $(plankton hash out.lst) $(plankton hash out2.lst) --via sha256:<normaliserProtocolRef>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Discover another session's results without a server:
.RS
.nf

  plankton mirror ../session-1/plankton-data
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR nekton (1),
.BR kton (1)
.PP
nekton records signed statements ABOUT fotons (reviews, findings, validations). kton is the
cockpit that serves/mirrors over the network and anchors to a transparency log.
